<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"/><meta name="description" content="Why Glassmacs exists, its debt to Aquamacs, its name, and its AI-assisted development."/><title>{title}</title><link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg"/><style>:root{font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",sans-serif;color-scheme:light dark}body{margin:0;background:#f6f8fc;color:#182b42;line-height:1.75}header,main,footer{max-width:780px;margin:auto;padding:20px 26px}header{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px}a{color:#25628c}h1{font-size:clamp(2.5rem,6vw,4rem);line-height:1.1;letter-spacing:-.05em}h2{margin-top:2.4em;line-height:1.2}p{font-size:1.09rem}blockquote{border-left:4px solid #25628c;padding:12px 22px;margin:30px 0;background:#e8f2f9}footer{border-top:1px solid #dce5ef;margin-top:60px}@media(prefers-color-scheme:dark){body{background:#101b2a;color:#ecf3fb}a{color:#8ec9f0}blockquote{background:#22394d}}</style></head><body><header><a href="/">← Glassmacs</a><nav><a href="/roadmap/">Feature roadmap</a> · <a href="https://github.com/jmping/Glassmacs">Source</a></nav></header><main><p><strong>Origins · An independent open-source experiment</strong></p><h1>Why Glassmacs exists</h1><p>For years, Aquamacs offered something special: the depth of GNU Emacs with the gestures, windows, menus, and document habits of a Mac application. For researchers writing R code alongside prose, that combination mattered.</p><h2>The problem was not Emacs</h2><p>Over time, the Aquamacs experience became harder to carry forward. The accumulated compatibility and integration layers could feel heavier and less responsive than the editing experience they were meant to support. More importantly, the version in use stopped working reliably on newer macOS releases, including the macOS release referred to during this project's development as “Golden Gate.” That made an everyday research tool difficult to depend on.</p><p>This is a description of the motivation for Glassmacs, not a claim that Aquamacs has no ongoing value or that every user encountered the same problems.</p><h2>Contribute upstream, or start a thinner layer?</h2><p>The initial question was whether to repair and modernize Aquamacs itself or create a new Mac-oriented distribution around current GNU Emacs. Contributing to the established project had obvious advantages: years of design decisions, compatibility work, and an existing community. But the maintenance question mattered. A deeply customized editor can make every upstream update a larger project.</p><p>Glassmacs chose the second path: keep GNU Emacs as the engine, avoid carrying a separate Emacs core, and implement the Mac-facing behavior in modular Emacs Lisp and small native integrations. The aim is not to rebuild every historical feature immediately. It is to make updating the underlying editor simpler while recovering the behaviors that made Aquamacs useful.</p><h2>An ode to Aquamacs</h2><blockquote>Aquamacs showed that Emacs did not have to ask Mac users to leave their desktop conventions at the door.</blockquote><p>Its contribution was more than a bundle or a theme. It treated document windows, menu discoverability, familiar shortcuts, fonts, and research-oriented workflows as first-class design concerns. It introduced many users to Emacs who might never have adopted a traditional Emacs setup. Glassmacs is possible in part because Aquamacs demonstrated the value of that approach.</p><p>Glassmacs is not affiliated with Aquamacs, and it is not presented as its official successor. The goal is to honor the idea while taking a different maintenance route.</p><h2>Why “Glassmacs”?</h2><p>The name pairs <em>glass</em>—a nod to the contemporary Mac interface and the idea of a clear pane onto powerful tools—with <em>macs</em>, a deliberate echo of the Mac-and-Emacs naming tradition that Aquamacs helped establish. It signals continuity in the user experience, not continuity of the codebase.</p><h2>Built by conversation, tested by machines and people</h2><p>Glassmacs has been developed through an intentionally AI-assisted, conversational workflow—often called <em>vibe coding</em>. The same is true of the text and implementation of this website. Human direction sets priorities, makes architectural decisions, and reviews outcomes; coding agents help draft code, tests, and documentation. The public commit and pull-request history makes that process inspectable.</p><p>That origin is not a guarantee of quality. It makes validation especially important: automated unit and integration tests, packaged macOS GUI acceptance, explicit regression cases, and eventually clean-machine beta testing. Recent work has included correcting acceptance-harness assumptions rather than mistaking every failing test for a product defect.</p><h2>Where it goes next</h2><p>Glassmacs starts with dependable documents, macOS window behavior, and R/ESS workflows. The <a href="/roadmap/">feature and parity roadmap</a> separates integrated work from work still being validated, missing Aquamacs behaviors, and longer-term ideas such as SSH, Git, containers, and Jupyter.</p></main><footer>Glassmacs · Independent project · <a href="/">Home</a></footer></body></html>